# UDP (User Datagram Protocol)

## What is UDP?
- UDP = User Datagram Protocol.
- Transport Layer (Layer 4) protocol.
- Connectionless.
- Fast but unreliable.

---

# Characteristics

- No connection establishment.
- No acknowledgements (ACK).
- No retransmissions.
- No ordering of packets.
- No flow control.
- No congestion control.
- Small header (8 bytes).

---

# UDP Header

| Field | Size |
|--------|------|
| Source Port | 2 Bytes |
| Destination Port | 2 Bytes |
| Length | 2 Bytes |
| Checksum | 2 Bytes |

Total Header Size = **8 Bytes**

---

# Data Unit

UDP → Datagram

---

# Advantages

- Low latency
- Low overhead
- Faster than TCP
- Suitable for real-time communication

---

# Disadvantages

- Packet loss possible
- Out-of-order delivery
- No guarantee of delivery
- No duplicate detection

---

# Common UDP Protocols

| Protocol | Port |
|----------|------|
| DNS | 53 |
| DHCP | 67, 68 |
| NTP | 123 |
| TFTP | 69 |
| SNMP | 161 |
| RTP (Voice/Video) | Dynamic |

